(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-premalignant-and-malignant-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pre-Malignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What does the deck say about amelanotic nail melanoma?","choices":["It occurs only in patients who already have multiple pigmented bands across several nails, so a single unexplained lesion can be observed","It is a benign variant of longitudinal melanonychia requiring no biopsy, provided the nail plate itself remains intact and unsplit","It may appear red, pink, eroded or mass-like without a dark band, and the absence of pigment does NOT exclude melanoma","It always shows at least a faint pigmented band somewhere in the nail, so the complete absence of pigment reliably excludes melanoma from the differential"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a pigment-free nail lesion can still be melanoma, so consider biopsy for any unexplained, progressive single-nail lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What is the most important distinction to make when assessing an actinic keratosis, and what raises that concern?","choices":["Solar lentigo — raised when a lesion is uniformly pigmented, flat, non-indurated, painless and stable over many years","Early squamous cell carcinoma or carcinoma in situ — raised when a lesion is thick, indurated, ulcerated, enlarging, painful, bleeding, persistent or recurrent","Seborrheic keratosis — raised when a lesion is thick, waxy, stuck-on, sharply demarcated, enlarging, persistent or recurrent","Superficial basal cell carcinoma — raised when a lesion is pearly with rolled borders, visible telangiectasias, ulceration, bleeding or slow enlargement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that list is the biopsy trigger. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where does cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma rank among skin cancers, and what usually causes it?","choices":["The second most common form of skin cancer, usually caused by long-term damage from ultraviolet rays from the sun or tanning beds","The most common form of skin cancer, usually caused by intense intermittent ultraviolet exposure in fair-skinned people","The fourth most common cancer overall, usually caused by human herpesvirus 8 in an immunosuppressed host","The rarest form of skin cancer, usually caused by abnormal T cells migrating into the skin over years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and when found early it is almost always curable. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What does the deck say you should do before assigning a diagnosis to a skin lesion?","choices":["Biopsy every pigmented lesion before any description of type, colour, texture, border or evolution, since morphology is unreliable in premalignant disease","Characterise it systematically — primary lesion type, colour, surface texture, border definition, size, distribution, palpability, ulceration, bleeding, induration and temporal evolution","Assign the most likely diagnosis first, then look for the features that confirm it — colour, texture, border, size and evolution — and discard those that do not fit","Photograph the lesion and defer all description — type, colour, texture, border, size, distribution and evolution — until the dermatology referral has been accepted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — describe before naming. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which variable does the deck call the dominant prognostic factor in melanoma, and what modifies it?","choices":["Patient age, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying stage-based prognosis","The anatomic site of the primary, with ulceration and mitotic activity further modifying prognosis","Breslow thickness, which must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy; ulceration and mitotic activity further modify stage-based prognosis","Lesion diameter, which must be accurately measured at the time of initial biopsy; ulceration and mitotic activity further modify prognosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — which is why the initial biopsy technique matters. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What proportion of patients develop a second basal cell carcinoma, and what does that mandate?","choices":["Up to 5%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Up to 50%, which makes examination every five years sufficient","Under 1%, which makes routine surveillance unnecessary after excision","Up to 50%, which makes at least annual full-skin examination mandatory"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the second-primary risk is what drives surveillance. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What recurrence and cure figures does the deck give for basal cell carcinoma surgery?","choices":["Excision recurrence rate of about 30%; Mohs cure rate of about 70%","Excision recurrence rate of 5% or less; Mohs cure rate of about 80%","Excision recurrence rate of about 20%; Mohs cure rate of about 98%","Excision recurrence rate of 5% or less; Mohs cure rate of about 98%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Mohs is the higher-cure, tissue-sparing option. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What metastatic rate does the deck give for actinically induced squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["An estimated 3 to 7%","An estimated 30 to 70%","An estimated 0.1 to 0.5%","An estimated 15 to 20%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — substantially higher with high-risk features. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What caveat does the deck attach to the 6 mm diameter criterion?","choices":["The threshold applies only to patients over 50","Smaller lesions can be melanoma","Larger lesions are never melanoma","The threshold applies only to lesions on the trunk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the threshold is a prompt, not a rule-out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which indications does the deck give for Mohs surgery in basal cell carcinoma?","choices":["Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and low-risk histology — superficial or nodular disease","The trunk, back, chest and shoulders; first presentations only; a purely cosmetic need; and any pigmented lesion whatever its histology","Any lesion in a patient over 65; any lesion larger than 6 mm; any lesion present more than a year; and any lesion the patient finds unsightly","Eyelids, nasolabial folds, canthi, external ear and temple; recurrent tumours; a tissue-sparing or cosmetic need; and aggressive histology — morpheaform, micronodular or infiltrative"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four groupings, with the histologic subtypes named. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"Describe the actinic keratosis lesion, including the finding the deck says may be more apparent by touch than by sight.","choices":["Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm pearly translucent or pink papules with visible telangiectasias that become more apparent when the skin is stretched","Small violaceous or purple macules and plaques, flesh-coloured early on, that are softer than surrounding skin and more apparent by sight than by touch","Small 0.2 to 0.6 cm flesh-coloured, pink or slightly hyperpigmented papules with a characteristic sandpaper texture on palpation","Large 2 to 6 cm deeply pigmented plaques, flesh-coloured at the edge, with a smooth waxy stuck-on surface that is more apparent by sight than by touch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the sandpaper texture is often felt before it is seen. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which basal cell carcinoma subtype is scar-like and carries the highest risk of subclinical spread?","choices":["Superficial — a reddish shiny scaly thin plaque whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Nodular — a pearly papule with central erosion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Pigmented — a stippled lesion whose extension beyond the visible segment is clinically subtle","Morpheaform or sclerosing — a scar-like or ivory-white lesion whose extension beyond the visible pink segment is clinically subtle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the invisible extension is what makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How does organ transplantation affect squamous cell carcinoma risk, and when do multiple tumours typically appear?","choices":["It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is uncommon and usually indolent in transplant recipients, with single tumours typically emerging about 5 years after transplant","It is common and often aggressive in transplant recipients, with multiple tumours typically emerging within 6 weeks of transplant","It is unchanged by transplantation, since immunosuppressive therapy does not influence keratinocyte carcinoma risk"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — chronic lymphocytic leukaemia and human immunodeficiency virus also raise risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which examination priorities does the deck list for a suspected malignant cutaneous lesion?","choices":["Dermoscopy alone, since the total-body survey, the palms, soles and nails, the oral mucosa and the regional nodes all belong to the dermatologist rather than the referring clinician","Good lighting, dermoscopy when trained, and a total-body skin survey when indicated; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when Kaposi sarcoma is possible; regional nodes for invasive or high-risk disease","Good lighting and dermoscopy in every patient regardless of training; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa in every patient; a total-body survey reserved for those who already have a skin cancer diagnosis","Wood lamp examination and potassium hydroxide preparation of any scale; palms, soles and nails; oral mucosa when tinea is possible; regional nodes in every patient regardless of risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — note the oral mucosa is tied specifically to Kaposi sarcoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the deck say about amputation in nail unit melanoma?","choices":["It is reserved for benign nail unit tumours such as glomus tumour or onychomatricoma that recur after local removal","It is NOT automatic — digit-sparing wide excision or Mohs with immunostaining is contemporary care, with amputation reserved for deep, extensive or bone-involving disease","It is the automatic first treatment for any nail unit melanoma whatever the depth or extent, since margins cannot be reliably assessed in the nail unit","It is never indicated at all, since digit-sparing wide excision clears every nail unit melanoma regardless of depth, extent or bone involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the digit is preserved where margins can be reliably assessed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"Describe NODULAR basal cell carcinoma, including the manoeuvre that accentuates its vessels.","choices":["A scar-like ivory-white plaque with subtle extension beyond the visible segment, accentuated by stretching the skin","A papule or nodule with central erosion, growing slowly over years to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly or translucent quality and visible telangiectasias accentuated by STRETCHING the skin","A papule or nodule with central erosion, growing rapidly over weeks to 1 to 2 cm or larger, with a pearly quality and telangiectasias accentuated by pressing the lesion firmly","A reddish shiny scaly thin plaque on the back or chest, growing slowly, with a thready pearly border accentuated by stretching the skin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — stretching brings the telangiectasias out. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which is the MOST COMMON malignant nail tumour, and what is it associated with?","choices":["Glomus tumour, associated with chronic inflammation or trauma, immunosuppression, prior radiation and older age","Nail unit squamous cell carcinoma or Bowen disease, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age","Nail unit melanoma, associated with high-risk human papillomavirus, immunosuppression, chronic inflammation or trauma, prior radiation, and older age, and often multifocal","Nail unit basal cell carcinoma, associated with cumulative ultraviolet exposure, fair skin, chronic trauma, prior radiation and older age"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — periungual disease may be multifocal. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What is the early morphology of cutaneous T-cell lymphoma, and what is it commonly mistaken for?","choices":["Localised or generalised erythematous patches or scaly plaques, usually on the trunk and frequently larger than 5 cm; it may resemble psoriasis, eczema or tinea","Localised pearly papules with telangiectasias, usually on the face and frequently smaller than 5 mm; it may resemble sebaceous hyperplasia","Red or purple macules and nodules, usually on the lower limbs and oral mucosa; it may resemble bacillary angiomatosis","Rough sandpaper papules on sun-exposed skin, usually under 6 mm; it may resemble seborrheic keratosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — which is why diagnosis is often delayed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What lifetime melanoma risk does the deck give by skin colour?","choices":["About 2% in white individuals; an equal 2% in persons of colour","About 0.1 to 0.5% in white individuals; about 2% in persons of colour","About 2% in white individuals; 0.1 to 0.5% in persons of colour","About 20% in white individuals; 1 to 5% in persons of colour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — lower risk in persons of colour, but not zero. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Approximately what proportion of actinic keratosis lesions progresses to squamous cell carcinoma each year?","choices":["About 1 in 1,000 lesions per year","About 1 in 10 lesions per year","About 1 in 100 lesions per year","About 1 in 100,000 lesions per year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and cumulative field risk matters more than any single lesion. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which findings does the deck list as urgent referral triggers in the nail unit?","choices":["New or changing single-digit longitudinal melanonychia, proximal widening, Hutchinson sign, or nail dystrophy with pigment","Longitudinal pigmented bands present in several nails since childhood, unchanged in width, colour, thickness and spacing over many years, with no nail dystrophy","Any nail change at all in a patient over 65, and any nail that has been abnormal for more than a month, whatever the appearance or the number of digits involved","Only a subungual mass confirmed on imaging, since clinical features such as pigment, widening, dystrophy or bleeding cannot themselves indicate urgency"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the list also includes an unexplained subungual mass, persistent ulceration or bleeding, and a chronic wart or infection that fails appropriate therapy. That failed-wart history is the one most easily dismissed. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What does Kaposi sarcoma look like, and which examination does the deck call essential when it is suspected?","choices":["Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin or mucous membranes; ORAL examination is essential, since hard palate lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Red or purple macules, plaques or nodules on skin only; NAIL examination is essential, since subungual lesions are common and may be the presenting site","Pearly translucent papules with telangiectasias; ocular examination is essential, since eyelid lesions are the usual presenting site","Erythematous scaly patches and plaques on the trunk; scalp examination is essential, since follicular involvement is the presenting sign"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the mouth may be where it declares itself. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype is described as rapidly growing, often amelanotic, and may LACK the classic recognition features?","choices":["Nodular melanoma","Superficial spreading melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it may lack ABCDE features, which makes it high-risk. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What follow-up schedule does the deck specify after squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":["Examination every five years, at closer intervals only for patients who have had more than ten tumours","No scheduled follow-up once the lesion has been completely excised with clear histologic margins","At least annual skin AND lymph-node examination, at closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients","At least annual skin examination alone, with lymph nodes assessed only once metastasis is suspected clinically"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the node examination is part of the routine, not an extra. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"At what Breslow thickness is sentinel lymph node biopsy offered or discussed, and what lowers that threshold?","choices":["At 4.0 mm or greater; or at 2.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration or high mitotic rate","At any thickness once the diagnosis is confirmed, since thickness does not influence the decision to stage the nodes","At 1.0 mm or greater; or at 0.8 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion","At 0.8 mm or greater; or at 1.0 mm or greater when additional histologic risk factors are present — ulceration, high mitotic rate, or lymphovascular invasion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — three risk factors lower the threshold. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which melanoma subtype is the most common, and what proportion of cases does it account for?","choices":["Lentigo maligna, about two-thirds of cases, arising on chronically sun-exposed skin of older adults","Acral lentiginous, about two-thirds of cases, arising on palms, soles and nail units regardless of sun exposure","Superficial spreading, about two-thirds of cases, arising on intermittently sun-exposed skin and evolving radially before vertical growth","Nodular, about two-thirds of cases, arising on intermittently sun-exposed skin and growing vertically from the outset"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — radial before vertical growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is the first priority in acquired immunodeficiency syndrome-associated Kaposi sarcoma?","choices":["Begin liposomal doxorubicin and paclitaxel — systemic chemotherapy is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin intralesional vinblastine and radiation — local palliation is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Reduce immunosuppressive medication doses — dose reduction is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease","Begin or optimise antiretroviral therapy — immune restoration is the cornerstone of treatment for epidemic disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — antiretroviral therapy comes before local measures. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What causes Kaposi sarcoma, and which cells does it arise from?","choices":["Malignant T cells migrating into the skin over years, arising in the lymphoid tissue","Human herpesvirus 8 combined with a weakened immune system, arising in the cells lining blood and lymph vessels","Human papillomavirus combined with a weakened immune system, arising in the keratinocytes of the epidermis","Ultraviolet damage combined with fair skin, arising in the basal cells of the epidermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the virus alone is not sufficient. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"When is field-directed therapy indicated for actinic keratosis, and which options does the deck list as most effective?","choices":["When a single lesion has clear borders and has not recurred; liquid nitrogen cryotherapy, curettage and electrodesiccation, Mohs micrographic surgery, and excision with 5 mm margins","When the patient is immunosuppressed or transplanted; oral acitretin, systemic methotrexate, photodynamic therapy, and methotrexate plus calcipotriene as a possible benefit","When the lesion has recurred twice after cryotherapy; radiation therapy, hedgehog pathway inhibitors, programmed death 1 blockade, and cetuximab as a possible benefit","When multiple lesions are present in one anatomic region, reflecting field cancerization risk; topical fluorouracil, imiquimod, photodynamic therapy, and fluorouracil plus calcipotriene as a possible benefit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — four options, the last flagged as possible rather than established. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Where does melanoma rank among cancers in the United States, and what is its standing among skin diseases?","choices":["The most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The 4th most common cancer, but a rare cause of death compared with squamous cell carcinoma","The 2nd most common skin cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease","The 4th most common cancer, and the leading cause of death due to skin disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — incidence doubled over the preceding 30 years. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 40"}]};
